\alttext(A histogram of name-field lengths, with most fields a few dozen bytes long and a tail of longer names.)

> The language, precisely

Matching is case-sensitive and anchored to the complete field. A star `*`
matches zero or more bytes; `?` matches exactly one. Every other pattern
byte is literal, including period, comma, slash, brackets and backslash.
There is no escaping, bracket class, path rule or case folding. This is a
restricted glob language, not a regular expression, a full shell expansion
engine, SQL LIKE or the complete `.gitignore` syntax.

Consequently `Mr.*` requires the field to begin with the literal bytes
`Mr.`. `*Mr.*` permits a prefix and suffix and asks whether those literal
bytes occur anywhere. A question mark refers to a byte, not a Unicode
character: UTF-8 `é` needs two question marks. Embedded NUL bytes remain
ordinary text bytes. If text contains `*`, a pattern star still acts as a
wildcard. For example, text `*ba` matches pattern `*a`; interpreting their
first equal-looking stars as a literal match would incorrectly reject it.
The canonical loop now handles the pattern star before literal comparison.

> A last-star scan and why only one retry point is needed

Use text offset \(i\), pattern offset \(j\), most recent star position
\(s\), and its current text endpoint \(q\). At a literal or question
match, advance both offsets. At a pattern star, remember it and first try
an empty match. On a mismatch, if a star exists, extend its matched text
by one and restart immediately after that star. Otherwise reject.
Trailing pattern stars may accept an empty suffix.

Between stars the pattern consists of fixed-length blocks of literals
and question marks. Choosing the earliest feasible placement of a block
leaves at least as much suffix text as any later placement. Once another
star is reached, that newer star can absorb any extra gap needed later;
there is no benefit in retaining alternative placements before it. This
exchange argument justifies remembering the most recent retry point.
It does not claim that all text positions are examined only once.

>> A separate prefix dynamic program describes the same language

Let \(D(i,j)\) mean that pattern prefix \([0,j)\) accepts text prefix
\([0,i)\). Start with \(D(0,0)=1\), \(D(i,0)=0\) for positive
\(i\), and \(D(0,j)=D(0,j-1)\) only for a trailing star in that
empty-text prefix. For positive indices,

$$
D(i,j)=
\begin{cases}
D(i,j-1)\lor D(i-1,j), & p_{j-1}=*,\\
D(i-1,j-1), & p_{j-1}=?\text{ or }p_{j-1}=t_{i-1},\\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(A star either matches empty text, using the state to its left, or consumes another byte, using the state above. A question mark or equal literal advances diagonally. Other cases are false.)

The star case has priority over literal equality, including when the text
byte itself is a star. Induction on prefix lengths proves the recurrence:
the final action of any accepting match is one of the listed cases, and
each listed predecessor extends to a valid match. The whole field is
accepted exactly when \(D(n,m)\) is true. This explanatory grid is not
the state path executed by the greedy matcher.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wildcard_prefixes.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto text = dm::datasets::titanic().string_at("Name",2);
  const std::string pattern = "*Miss.*";
  const auto work = dm::algorithms::wildcard_match_stats(text,pattern);
  std::print("| field | pattern | matched | scan steps | literal tests | retries |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n"
      "| {} | `{}` | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",text,pattern,work.matched,work.steps,work.literal_tests,work.retries);
  std::print("\nActual scan transitions (first 14):\n\n{}\n",dm::algorithms::wildcard_match_trace(text,pattern)
      .select({"event","action","text_before","pattern_before","text_after","pattern_after"}).head(14).to_md());
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::algorithms::wildcard_dp_plot(text,pattern).title("Prefix states for a passenger name and *Miss.*")
      .size(1000,900).save_svg("build/wildcard_prefixes.svg");
}
```
