\caption(Record counts, actual scan work, a sample filtered frame and observational survival summaries for four title-like patterns. Original rows and columns are retained by the filter.)
\alttext(A bar chart showing Mr as the largest wrapped title filter, followed by Miss, Mrs and Master, smaller William and Anna matches, and zero matches for the unwrapped prefix and lowercase mr patterns.)

The wrapped and unwrapped `Mr.` patterns show the effect of anchoring;
the lowercase query shows case sensitivity. Survival fractions describe
these matched records and their associated demographics. They do not
establish a causal title effect or turn the glob into a survival model.
Null strings are distinct from empty strings: a null is skipped, whereas
an empty string is a valid field matched by an empty pattern or stars.

> Why monotone star expansion does not prove linear time

The star endpoint \(q\) only increases, but the current text offset
\(i\) can return to the beginning of the suffix being retried. Take
text \(a^{2r}\) and pattern \(*a^r b\). For each failed candidate,
the matcher consumes \(r\) matching `a` bytes and tests the final `b`
again. There are \(r\) such failing suffix attempts, then a final
\(r\)-byte scan reaches text exhaustion with `b` still unmatched.
Including the initial star transition, the exact loop-step count is

$$
C(r)=1+r(r+1)+r=(r+1)^2.
$$
\alttext(One initial star step, r retries each requiring r successful comparisons and a failing comparison, and one final r-byte scan give exactly the square of r plus one steps.)

Both input lengths grow linearly with \(r\), so this family is
quadratic. The general scan bound is \(O((n+1)(m+1))\): at most
\(n\) star expansions, each allowing another pattern suffix scan, plus
the initial and trailing work. It avoids exponential enumeration of star
assignments, yet constant auxiliary storage is compatible with repeated
work. Optimised glob implementations may use additional strategies; this
bound describes this actual local loop.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wildcard_quadratic.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::vector<double> text_lengths,steps,formula;
  std::print("| r | text bytes | pattern bytes | accepted | steps | literal tests | retries | (r+1)^2 |\n"
      "| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const std::size_t r : {8,16,32,64,128,256}) {
    const std::string text(2*r,'a'),pattern="*"+std::string(r,'a')+"b";
    const auto work = dm::algorithms::wildcard_match_stats(text,pattern);
    text_lengths.push_back(text.size());steps.push_back(work.steps);formula.push_back((r+1)*(r+1));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",r,text.size(),pattern.size(),work.matched,
        work.steps,work.literal_tests,work.retries,(r+1)*(r+1));
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(text_lengths,formula,"formula",dm::RGB{100,100,100},2)
      .points(text_lengths,steps,"counted steps",dm::RGB{37,99,235},5)
      .title("The last-star scan has a quadratic worst-case family")
      .x_label("text length (2r bytes)").y_label("greedy-loop steps").y_limits(0,80000)
      .size(1000,480).save_svg("build/wildcard_quadratic.svg");
}
```
