// result_end
\caption(Actual work agrees exactly with the derived count. These synthetic strings isolate the mechanism; they are not representative passenger names or a wall-clock benchmark.)
\alttext(A rising quadratic work curve with measured step counts on the theoretical reference line as text length increases.)

The full explanatory DP takes \(O((n+1)(m+1))\) storage as well as time.
Its public helper checks a cell budget before allocating. The trace helper
also checks its retained-event budget. The ordinary decision and counter
APIs need constant auxiliary storage and do not build either diagnostic
artifact.

> Conclusion

Whole-field glob filters are precise about anchoring, byte consumption,
case and punctuation. On Titanic names they produce reproducible row
subsets and record-level summaries. A shared scan gives both decisions
and actual work traces; a separate DP explains acceptance independently.
Correct pattern-star handling includes stars inside the text. Finally,
an exact adversarial count makes the performance limit explicit: this
last-star matcher uses constant workspace but can take quadratic time.

> References

- K. J. Krauss. *Matching Wildcards: An Improved Algorithm for Big Data*.
  https://developforperformance.com/MatchingWildcards_AnImprovedAlgorithmForBigData.html
- R. Cox (2017). *Glob Matching Can Be Simple And Fast Too*.
  https://research.swtch.com/glob
- Python standard-library documentation, *fnmatch*: the broader shell-style
  syntax and case-sensitive `fnmatchcase` operation.
  https://docs.python.org/3/library/fnmatch.html
